12:33:47  INFO  [msg ← ISP] Diagnosed. This is not a stale blackhole — my routing table is clean and my forwarding decision for a packet sourced from 45.32.0.1 correctly points out toward TransitAS. I reproduced your symptom locally:

  - ping -I 85.12.64.1 (my loopback, globally advertised)   → 44.192.100.100: 2/2 ✓
  - ping -I 10.0.32.1 (my link IP to you, NOT advertised)   → 44.192.100.100: 0/2 ✗
  - Same forward path (traceroute from loopback: 3 hops to target; traceroute from link IP: 10/10 stars, not even TTL-exceededs coming back).

The forward path is fine. The problem is the **return path**: AWS has no route back to your source IP. So the ICMP echo-reply (and even intermediate TTL-exceededs) never make it back.

Two candidate root causes for your 45.32.0.0/24 being unreachable from AWS:

  1) TransitAS isn't re-advertising your /24 to their upstream, or their upstream is filtering it.
  2) More likely: 45.32.0.0/24 has an AltDB IRR object (origin AS-TINYINC, submitted 2021-03-10) but **no RPKI ROA**. Any Tier1 doing strict RPKI-ROV that treats "not-found" as reject-worthy, or that filters IRR-only prefixes with weak sources like AltDB, will drop your announcement. Given this whole incident has been about how untrustworthy AltDB is (an attacker forged AltDB objects for AWS space), it's very plausible your Tier1 or AWS's Tier1 has tightened IRR-based acceptance and your AltDB-only /24 is caught in the crossfire.

Fix path:
  (a) I've asked TransitAS to check whether they're re-advertising 45.32.0.0/24 upstream and whether it's visible in their upstream's RIB. Waiting on that.
  (b) Strongly recommend you publish an RPKI ROA for 45.32.0.0/24 with origin AS-TINYINC via your RIR (ARIN/RIPE/whoever holds your allocation). That's the only durable fix — ROAs are cryptographically authoritative and won't be filtered.
  (c) As a workaround only if (a) shows the announcement isn't propagating: you can source tests from within a globally-reachable prefix if you have one, but I'd rather solve (b) properly.

